  <Document>
    <name>🌫️ LIVE AIR QUALITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 7, 2026 6:04AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 7, 2026 7:04AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE AIR QUALITY</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (CAMS analysis), hourly check</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/air_quality/legend.png?v=cams-pm25-2026-10-07T10-00-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>PM2.5 concentration in micrograms per cubic meter (CAMS model analysis, fixed EPA-anchored scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> green good &rarr; <b>12 moderate</b> yellow &rarr; <b>35.4 unhealthy for sensitive groups</b> orange &rarr; <b>55.4 unhealthy</b> red &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 150</b> purple very unhealthy (above clamps into purple). Same concentration always shows the same color. Modeled PM2.5 — not aerosol optical depth, not station observations.</p><p><b>Source:</b> ECMWF CAMS European air-quality analysis via Open-Meteo Air Quality API (PM2.5, no key, hourly)<br/><a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/air-quality-api">https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/air-quality-api</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE AIR QUALITY</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 7, 2026 6:04AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 7, 2026 7:04AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE AIR QUALITY</h2><p>Near-surface fine-particulate (PM2.5) conditions from the CAMS atmospheric-composition analysis: a continuous field flagging wildfire-smoke intrusions, urban haze, and regional pollutant transport over the Great Lakes. Low values mark clean air; orange, red, and purple mark progressively degraded, unhealthy air. The underlying variable is PM2.5, identified as modeled output (CAMS analysis), not station observations.</p><p>Air-quality science (atmospheric chemistry and public-health exposure assessment) tracks PM2.5, particles under 2.5 micrometers that penetrate deep into lungs. Concentrations in micrograms per cubic meter map to health-based breakpoints (good, moderate, unhealthy for sensitive groups, unhealthy, very unhealthy). This layer shows modeled concentration, not aerosol optical depth, and is labeled as model output rather than station observations.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/air_quality/legend.png?v=cams-pm25-2026-10-07T10-00-g2" width="600" alt="key"><br>PM2.5 concentration in micrograms per cubic meter (CAMS model analysis, fixed EPA-anchored scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> green good &rarr; <b>12 moderate</b> yellow &rarr; <b>35.4 unhealthy for sensitive groups</b> orange &rarr; <b>55.4 unhealthy</b> red &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 150</b> purple very unhealthy (above clamps into purple). Same concentration always shows the same color. Modeled PM2.5 — not aerosol optical depth, not station observations.<br><b>Units:</b> ug/m3 PM2.5 (display; source ug/m3 as filed)<br><b>Source:</b> ECMWF CAMS European air-quality analysis via Open-Meteo Air Quality API (PM2.5, no key, hourly)<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only when CAMS publishes a newer valid hour.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/air-quality-api">https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/air-quality-api</a></p>]]></description>
